##############################################################################
# Milo v1.5 — LinuxCNC HAL for Remora over SPI
#
# Pi 5 (rp1lib) <-- SPI --> BTT Octopus v1.1 running Remora firmware
#
#   Octopus PA_7  MOSI  <-> Pi header pin 19  (GPIO 10)
#   Octopus PA_6  MISO  <-> Pi header pin 21  (GPIO  9)
#   Octopus PA_5  SCK   <-> Pi header pin 23  (GPIO 11)
#   Octopus PA_4  SSEL  <-> Pi header pin 24  (GPIO  8)
#   Octopus GND         <-> Pi header pin 20
#   Octopus PC_15 RESET <-> Pi header pin 22  (GPIO 25)
# GPIO 25 is HARDCODED in remora-spi.c (reset_gpio_pin), not a parameter.
# Without that wire the watchdog cannot reset the PRU after an SPI fault.
# The SPI link itself is already proven working on the bench.
#
# Pin assignments come from octopus/config.txt in this repo, which is the file
# that must be copied onto the OCTOPUS's own microSD card as "config.txt".
# The two must always agree — joint numbers, Data Bit numbers, base frequency.
#
# The RRF config's pin names (PC_7, PD_11, PB_10, PE_6, PB_9) were Fly-CDYv3
# pins and are USELESS here — the CDYv3 is out of the machine.
#
# ⚠️ DO NOT power the machine with this config until every TODO is resolved and
#    the endstop logic has been checked in halshow with the motors unpowered.
#    A wrong endstop polarity homes an axis straight into its hard stop.
##############################################################################

# --- Core motion -------------------------------------------------------------
loadrt [KINS]KINEMATICS
loadrt [EMCMOT]EMCMOT servo_period_nsec=[EMCMOT]SERVO_PERIOD num_joints=[KINS]JOINTS

# --- Remora ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Remora component detects the Pi generation at runtime and loads either the
# BCM2835 driver (Pi 4 and earlier) or rp1lib (Pi 5). Nothing to select here.
# PRU_base_freq MUST match the base frequency compiled into the Remora firmware
# on the Octopus (configuration.h). Stock is 40 kHz. Changing it here alone does
# not change the firmware — it only tells the component what the firmware does.
# 🚨 USE SPI_freq, NOT SPI_clk_div. Tested on the bench 2026-09-04: SPI_clk_div
#    is accepted but IGNORED — BAUDR stayed at 20 MHz for values 10/32/64/128.
#    SPI_freq does take effect (verified 10 M / 5 M / 2 M / 1 M).
#    Default with no parameter is 20 MHz, which is fast for a ribbon.
#    Start at 2 MHz; raise only once motion is proven, and back it off at the
#    first sign of SPI comms trouble.
# 40000 is CONFIRMED: PRU_BASEFREQ in Remora-OS6/configuration.h, and
# octopus/config.txt has no "Threads" override.
loadrt remora-spi SPI_freq=2000000 PRU_base_freq=40000

addf remora.read            servo-thread
addf motion-command-handler servo-thread
addf motion-controller      servo-thread
addf remora.update-freq     servo-thread
addf remora.write           servo-thread

# --- E-stop / SPI link health ------------------------------------------------
# remora.SPI-status goes false if the link to the Octopus drops, which drops
# machine power. This is the one safety net that comes for free — keep it.
net user-enable-out     iocontrol.0.user-enable-out     => remora.SPI-enable
net user-request-enable iocontrol.0.user-request-enable => remora.SPI-reset
net remora-status       remora.SPI-status               => iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in

##############################################################################
# 🚨 SAFETY LOOP GAP — LinuxCNC does not currently know the machine is dead
#
# Today emc-enable-in is driven by SPI-status ALONE. That covers "the link to
# the Octopus failed". It does NOT cover the e-stop, and here is why that
# matters with the contactor topology:
#
#   E-stop drops the contactor -> VFD mains and the Octopus VM stepper rail die.
#   The Octopus LOGIC stays live (deliberately — it keeps endstops and position).
#   So SPI keeps working, SPI-status stays TRUE, and LinuxCNC happily carries on
#   executing the program into dead drivers.
#
# Result: the tool stops moving but the program does not stop. Commanded
# position walks away from actual position, and you find out at reset.
#
# THE FIX: a normally-open auxiliary contact on the contactor, wired between
# an Octopus input and GND (dry contact — do NOT put 24 V on a 3.3 V input).
#   contactor energised -> contact closed -> input pulled LOW -> .not is TRUE
#   wire breaks         -> input floats HIGH -> .not is FALSE -> e-stop
# That polarity is deliberate: a broken wire reads as "not safe".
#
# ⚠️ LEFT COMMENTED because the contactor is NOT BUILT YET (as of 2026-09-06 the
#    machine is mains -> e-stop switch -> 24 V PSU, with no contactor at all).
#    Enabling this against an unwired PG_12 would read "not live" forever and
#    lock the machine in E-stop. Uncomment only once the aux contact is in.
##############################################################################

# loadrt and2 count=1
# addf and2.0 servo-thread
# net estop-ok      remora.input.04.not => and2.0.in0
# net remora-status remora.SPI-status   => and2.0.in1
# net machine-ok    and2.0.out          => iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in
#   ... and DELETE the direct 'net remora-status ... => iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in'
#   line above when you do, or HAL will refuse two drivers on one signal.

# remora.PRU-reset drives the hardware reset line (Pi GPIO 25 -> Octopus
# PC_15). Left unnetted: the component pulses it internally on SPI failure.

##############################################################################
# JOINTS
#
# CONFIRMED against octopus/config.txt: 0=X (MOTOR0), 1=Y (MOTOR1), 2=Z
# (MOTOR2) — which also matches RRF's M584 X0 Y1 Z2.
#
# NOTE: .scale and .maxaccel are HAL *parameters* (hal_param_float_newf),
# which is why they are set with setp and not net.
##############################################################################

# ---- Joint 0 : X ----
setp remora.joint.0.scale     [JOINT_0]SCALE
setp remora.joint.0.maxaccel  [JOINT_0]STEPGEN_MAXACCEL
net x-pos-cmd  joint.0.motor-pos-cmd  => remora.joint.0.pos-cmd
net x-pos-fb   remora.joint.0.pos-fb  => joint.0.motor-pos-fb
net x-enable   joint.0.amp-enable-out => remora.joint.0.enable

# ---- Joint 1 : Y ----
setp remora.joint.1.scale     [JOINT_1]SCALE
setp remora.joint.1.maxaccel  [JOINT_1]STEPGEN_MAXACCEL
net y-pos-cmd  joint.1.motor-pos-cmd  => remora.joint.1.pos-cmd
net y-pos-fb   remora.joint.1.pos-fb  => joint.1.motor-pos-fb
net y-enable   joint.1.amp-enable-out => remora.joint.1.enable

# ---- Joint 2 : Z ----
setp remora.joint.2.scale     [JOINT_2]SCALE
setp remora.joint.2.maxaccel  [JOINT_2]STEPGEN_MAXACCEL

##############################################################################
# STEPGEN DEADBAND — stops the stepgen hunting at standstill
#
# remora.joint.N.deadband defaults to 0.0, which makes the stepgen chase
# position errors far smaller than one step — errors it physically cannot
# correct. The result is a continuous ±1 step dither: freq-cmd alternating
# instead of settling, and the motor audibly buzzing while stationary.
#
# Measured 2026-09-06 with deadband=0: X freq-cmd alternated -1.07 / +1.93 and
# pos-fb dithered by exactly 0.005 mm = one step at 200 steps/mm. Y did the
# same (-1 / +2); it simply was not audible. Setting the deadband stopped both
# — Y went to 2.8e-09, X to a steady value with ZERO drift measured over 40 s.
#
# Set to 1.5 steps. This costs nothing in achievable accuracy, because the
# stepgen cannot resolve finer than one step in the first place:
#     X/Y  1 step = 1/200 = 0.005 mm   -> 0.0075
#     Z    1 step = 1/400 = 0.0025 mm  -> 0.00375
# The deadband is in MACHINE UNITS (mm), applied to command-minus-feedback.
##############################################################################
setp remora.joint.0.deadband  0.0075
setp remora.joint.1.deadband  0.0075
setp remora.joint.2.deadband  0.00375
net z-pos-cmd  joint.2.motor-pos-cmd  => remora.joint.2.pos-cmd
net z-pos-fb   remora.joint.2.pos-fb  => joint.2.motor-pos-fb
net z-enable   joint.2.amp-enable-out => remora.joint.2.enable

##############################################################################
# HOME / LIMIT SWITCHES
#
# RRF had exactly three switches, one per axis, all "S1" (active-high NO/NC
# switch, not a probe):
#     M574 X1 S1   -> X switch at the MIN end
#     M574 Y2 S1   -> Y switch at the MAX end
#     M574 Z2 S1   -> Z switch at the MAX end
#
# Each is wired as home-only here; travel is protected by the soft limits in
# milo.ini, which is why HOME_IGNORE_LIMITS is set on all three joints.
#
# Wired in octopus/config.txt as Digital Pin inputs on the Octopus's DIAG
# headers: X=PG_6 (DIAG0), Y=PG_9 (DIAG1), Z=PG_10 (DIAG2).
# Those pin numbers come from the standard Octopus v1.1 pinout, NOT from
# Remora's docs — check them against the board silkscreen.
# Verify in halshow that each pin reads FALSE with the switch open and TRUE
# when pressed by hand. If one reads inverted, swap remora.input.NN for
# remora.input.NN.not rather than editing the firmware.
##############################################################################

# Data Bit N in octopus/config.txt maps to remora.input.NN here.
# ⚠️ TWO DIGITS. remora-spi.c creates these as "%s.input.%02d", so the pin is
#    remora.input.00, NOT remora.input.0 — a one-digit name silently fails to
#    bind and homing never sees the switch.
# ⚠️ VERIFY IN HALSHOW, MOTORS UNPOWERED, before homing anything.
net x-home  remora.input.00 => joint.0.home-sw-in
net y-home  remora.input.01 => joint.1.home-sw-in
net z-home  remora.input.02 => joint.2.home-sw-in

##############################################################################
# SPINDLE — RRF: M950 R0 C"PE_6+!PB_9" L24000 Q100
#   L24000 = 24000 RPM full scale, Q100 = 100 Hz PWM, !PB_9 = inverted enable
#
# ⚠️ There is NO remora.PWM.* HAL pin. Confirmed from remora-spi.c: the only
#    pins it creates are SPI-enable/reset/status, PRU-reset, joint.N.*,
#    input.NN(.not), output.NN, SP.N and PV.N.
#
# Spindle speed therefore travels on a SETPOINT channel: LinuxCNC writes
# remora.SP.<n>, and the firmware's PWM module consumes that index.
#
# Firmware side (octopus/config.txt), once the expansion board is wired.
# Keys confirmed by reading the flashed FIRMWARE.CUR:
#     { "Thread": "Servo", "Type": "PWM",
#       "Comment":      "Spindle speed",
#       "SP":           0,
#       "PWM Pin":      "PB_6",
#       "PWM Max":      24000,
#       "Hardware PWM": "True" }
#   PB_6 is the Octopus servo/BLTouch pin — where the Milo docs route the
#   expansion board's PWM IN. Hardware PWM only works on timer-capable pins;
#   the firmware prints "Error: Hardware PWM cannot this pin" otherwise, so
#   read the boot output. "Variable Freq" is also supported if needed.
#
# Enable relay: a Digital Pin in Output mode -> remora.output.NN (two digits).
#
# TODO(wiring): nothing is connected to the expansion board or relay yet, so
# these stay commented. Confirm the physical pins before uncommenting.
##############################################################################

# net spindle-speed  spindle.0.speed-out-abs => remora.SP.0
# net spindle-on     spindle.0.on            => remora.output.00

# spindle.0.at-speed deliberately NOT wired to a placeholder. Without a real
# signal G-code plunges the moment M3 is issued, before the spindle spools up.
# See README-config.md — the answer is a VFD "up to frequency" output into a
# remora input, or Modbus if the drive is a Huanyang.

##############################################################################
# MIST COOLANT + AIR BLAST
#
# LinuxCNC drives these from G-code: M7 = mist on, M8 = flood on, M9 = both off.
# There is no flood system here, so M8 is repurposed as the air blast — which
# means you get independent G-code control of coolant and chip clearing.
#
# Firmware side (octopus/config.txt), both added 2026-09-08:
#   PA_8 (FAN0 MOSFET) -> remora.output.00 -> mist solenoid
#   PE_5 (FAN1 MOSFET) -> remora.output.01 -> air blast solenoid
#
# ⚠️ Use the FAN/HEATER MOSFET outputs, not a bare GPIO. Those are low-side
#    switches with gates pulled down, so they are OFF through boot and reset —
#    the same reasoning as the spindle enable relay. A bare 3.3 V pin cannot
#    drive a 24 V solenoid at all.
# ⚠️ PUT A FLYBACK DIODE ACROSS EACH SOLENOID COIL. An inductive load on a bare
#    MOSFET kills the FET on the first switch-off.
# ⚠️ Pin numbers are from the standard Octopus v1.1 pinout — verify against the
#    silkscreen, and confirm the modules parsed by reading the boot banner.
#
# Gating the air matters beyond tidiness: the CAT 8010 is rated 70/30 duty with
# a 60-minute continuous ceiling, so running blast continuously through a long
# job exceeds the compressor's own spec. On M8 it only flows during cuts.
##############################################################################

net coolant-mist  iocontrol.0.coolant-mist  => remora.output.00
net coolant-flood iocontrol.0.coolant-flood => remora.output.01

##############################################################################
# TOOLSETTER / TOUCH PROBE
#
# Both existed under RRF (toolsetter.g, touchprobe.g.example). Wire them once
# basic motion is proven — a probe on a machine that cannot yet home reliably
# is just a way to break a probe.
# TODO(pins)
##############################################################################

# Toolsetter is on STOP3 = PG_11 -> remora.input.03 (config.txt, Data Bit 3).
# ⚠️ Two digits: remora.input.03, not .3 — a one-digit name silently never binds.
# ⚠️ VERIFY IN HALSHOW before uncommenting: trigger the toolsetter by hand and
#    confirm the pin changes state. If it reads inverted, use remora.input.03.not
#    rather than editing the firmware.
# ⚠️ LinuxCNC has exactly ONE motion.probe-input. A touch probe added later must
#    share it — OR'd in HAL, or physically switched. Do not net two sources here.
# ✅ VERIFIED 2026-09-06: FALSE at rest, TRUE on contact. No invert needed.
net probe-in  remora.input.03 => motion.probe-input

# --- Tool change (manual) ----------------------------------------------------
net tool-change     iocontrol.0.tool-change     => iocontrol.0.tool-changed
net tool-prep       iocontrol.0.tool-prepare    => iocontrol.0.tool-prepared
